WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.140
I'm Jacqueline Nolis.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.440
This is my presentation.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:13.130
How one time I made an entire
e-commerce platform on Shiny.

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:15.620
They said it couldn't
be done, but I did it.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.360
What is that platform?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.800
That platform is ggirl,
which is to make

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.970
your ggplots in real life.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:26.540
Some people may pronounce it g-girl, but it is officially GG-IRL.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:30.130
You can install it via
remotes::install_github("jnolis/ggirl")

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:32.619
and what it does is it
lets you take a ggplot you've

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:35.020
made that's beautiful
that you wish existed

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:36.320
in the physical realm.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.880
You can then pay a modest
fee and very soon you

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.030
will get an actual postcard
delivered to a friend or family

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.830
member.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:46.730
But it's all cool
to talk about it.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.440
Let's actually see how it works

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:48.950
instead.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:50.950
So I'm going to immediately
switch out of PowerPoint

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.190
and switch to a live demo.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:53.900
So I'm running this
in Saturn cloud.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:55.730
I'm just going to
open up RStudio.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:57.280
And so let's say
you have a plot you

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.160
like let's say you have this
souvenir commemorative--

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:04.710
Oh, oh, no.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:09.152
I think we're also.

00:01:09.152 --> 00:01:10.110
Yeah, we're missing it.

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.770
Is it not showing up. Wait, here?

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:13.890
Can I do this?

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.148
Yeah cool.

00:01:15.148 --> 00:01:16.440
Good! That would've been tragic.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:17.357
This is the best part.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.260
Here we go.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:22.802
Let's see if I can
do this from over here.

00:01:22.802 --> 00:01:23.710
Yes! OK, great.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.143
Let's say you have this souvenir
commemorative ggirl plot.

00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:29.060
Oh, god, I'm going to
have to do it like this.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:29.780
Oh, no, I see it.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:30.280
We're good.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.070
OK, so you have the souvenir
commemorative ggirl plot.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:38.230
You can then go and you can use
this ggpostcard() function which

00:01:38.230 --> 00:01:41.285
takes a plot, the contact email
for the order confirmation,

00:01:41.285 --> 00:01:42.910
the message you want
to put on the back

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.990
of the postcard like "This
is a great conference,

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.500
Thanks for putting it on!" and the
address of a friend or family

00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:48.342
member. In this case,

00:01:48.342 --> 00:01:50.300
I'm going to send it to
our friends at RStudio--

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.865
I mean Posit--in
Boston, Massachusetts.

00:01:52.865 --> 00:01:54.490
So in several weeks,
their headquarters

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:56.490
is going to be confused
why they got a postcard.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.310
But here we are.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.863
So I'm going to hit that.

00:01:58.863 --> 00:02:00.280
And what's going
to happen is it's

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:01.780
going to think for
a second and then

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.340
it's going to launch an
entirely separate Shiny Server.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.355
This is a Shiny Server
running somewhere else.

00:02:06.355 --> 00:02:07.730
It is not running
on my computer.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.910
It is running actually
on Saturn cloud.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:12.440
So on the front you can see
the preview of the postcard.

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:13.580
It shows you the message.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:14.720
It shows you the address.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.350
It has one button and
that's "Pay and Submit"

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:20.260
when you hit Pay and Submit.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:21.302
Oh, god, this is so hard.

00:02:21.302 --> 00:02:21.802
There we go.

00:02:21.802 --> 00:02:22.310
OK, great.

00:02:22.310 --> 00:02:25.150
When you hit Pay and Submit,
it thinks for a second

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:26.600
and sends you to Stripe.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:27.975
In Stripe, it asks
you for money.

00:02:27.975 --> 00:02:30.600
So I'm going to go ahead and put
in my credit card information.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:31.160
Just kidding,

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.600
Cyber stalkers!

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:35.785
So you do that.

00:02:35.785 --> 00:02:38.410
And then if you did somehow put
in your credit card information

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.020
in front of a bunch of people,
you would then very soon

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:43.570
get an email confirming
that you made the order

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:46.480
and in one to two weeks you
will get a physical postcard

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:47.900
of your ggplot.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:48.710
That's right.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:53.500
The first presentation you've
seen with physical props.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:54.860
Your message is on the back.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.630
It's got the ggirl hex sticker.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:03:00.130
Oh, I think I just doxxed
myself and my address.

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.440
Cool OK, so now we're back
to PowerPoint and let's

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.010
see if I can pop this back up.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:06.520
Let me see.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.100
You got it?

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:07.670
OK, great.

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:09.140
Cool, cool.

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:10.070
So that was the demo.

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.450
As you saw, it went from R
to a Shiny app to Stripe

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:15.350
and then somehow a
postcard appeared.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:16.340
But how does it work?

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.415
How does it work?

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.500
How does it work?

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.350
There we go.

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:24.480
How does it work?

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.357
OK, so there's an
R package called ggirl.

00:03:27.357 --> 00:03:29.940
That's what you install on your
computer and it talks to the Shiny

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:30.490
Server.

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:33.483
And then there is a Shiny Server
called ggirl-server, which

00:03:33.483 --> 00:03:35.400
does all the work of
showing the user preview,

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.817
passing the
information to Stripe,

00:03:36.817 --> 00:03:39.147
fulfilling the
postcard via an API.

00:03:39.147 --> 00:03:41.730
So there's a service somewhere
I call to make the post card go

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.750
and then sends a
confirmation email.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:46.140
Now if you think
about it, ggirl-server

00:03:46.140 --> 00:03:49.830
should be multiple Shiny
apps and plumber APIs.

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.230
There is no reason why
the fulfillment, why

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.720
the uploading the information
should be done via Shiny.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.330
It's better, probably better to
do that as an API via plumber,

00:03:57.330 --> 00:03:59.400
but for ease of
maintenance, I'm like,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.840
I really just want one
Shiny app to do all of this.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.200
And by ease of maintenance,
I mean I'm super lazy

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.150
and I don't want to have to
keep a bunch of microservices

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:06.650
running.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:07.480
I just want one.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.150
But to make this
work leads a lot

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.420
of engineering questions, which
I'm going to enumerate now.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:16.500
One is how do you pass
the plot from R to Shiny?

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:18.000
If you think about
it--Shiny, there's

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.769
a lot of times where Shiny
has data loaded into it, right?

00:04:20.769 --> 00:04:23.020
You can read data from
a database in Shiny.

00:04:23.020 --> 00:04:25.013
You can press the
Upload button on Shiny.

00:04:25.013 --> 00:04:26.430
But if you think
about it, there's

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.430
not really an obvious way that
if a Shiny Server is running,

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:31.960
how can you programmatically
send data to it?

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.730
So I don't want it
to read data on load.

00:04:33.730 --> 00:04:36.220
I want to just pass the data
to a server that's running.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:37.810
It may not even have
a web page open.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.930
I just want it to
listen for data.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:44.220
So the way you would do that
in web development land is you

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.060
do an HTTP POST request.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:49.590
So an HTTP POST request is
the way an API or a website

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:50.680
receives data.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.320
So when you hit
Submit on a website,

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:54.360
your browser is
doing a POST request

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.610
to the server. That should
be done with a POST request.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.960
Shiny does not have the
ability--a documented ability

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.010
to listen for POST requests.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.190
Normally only plumber does that.

00:05:05.190 --> 00:05:06.670
It should have this.

00:05:06.670 --> 00:05:08.500
And that's my call
out to RStudio.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.290
I mean Posit. I think that would
be a really great feature.

00:05:11.290 --> 00:05:12.720
The good news is
there's actually

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.820
a number of undocumented
ways to do this.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.320
And so, again, what
I'm trying to do

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:18.610
is I'm trying to post
the image to Shiny.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:20.400
So here's the plot
I've made, and then I

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:22.390
want Shiny to come
back to me with OK.

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:23.970
Here's the ID of that image.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.470
I'm like, cool, I
got the image, I'm

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:27.225
going to call it fc, whatever.

00:05:27.225 --> 00:05:29.350
And then the package is
going to be like, OK, cool.

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:31.950
I'm then going to take the
user to the browser website

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:33.690
to actually do the preview.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.090
So this is a back and
forth of the POST request.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.890
So there are a number of
undocumented ways to do this.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:41.550
The way I like the most is
you use the brochure package

00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:42.580
by Colin Fay.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:44.250
This is an experimental
package that

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:47.910
lets you this is an experimental
package that lets you connect

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:49.273
multiple Shiny apps together.

00:05:49.273 --> 00:05:51.690
So if you have three different
Shiny apps and you're like,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.000
man, I really wish all of
them were running at once,

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.220
you can bundle them all up into
one Shiny app using brochure.

00:05:57.220 --> 00:05:59.797
You can also listen to
requests besides GET.

00:05:59.797 --> 00:06:01.380
So if you want to
have your Shiny app

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.087
listen for data, not just
show web pages, brochure

00:06:04.087 --> 00:06:05.170
will let you do that too.

00:06:05.170 --> 00:06:06.970
So this actually solves
two of my problems.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:09.570
One, it lets me listen
for POST data, and two,

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:12.450
I can actually have a bunch
of concurrent Shiny apps.

00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.820
One for the order upload,
one for the fulfillment,

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.830
one for the preview.

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:17.330
It does.

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:18.880
It's so new it doesn't
even have a hex sticker.

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.550
It also says all
over it "Experimental

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:21.695
do not use in production."

00:06:21.695 --> 00:06:23.070
But here I am
using in production

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:24.028
and it's working great.

00:06:24.028 --> 00:06:25.457
Thanks Colin.

00:06:25.457 --> 00:06:27.540
If you want to see examples
of the other ways that

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:29.790
are actually built into
Shiny to do POST request,

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:32.670
I have a link at the bottom
and you can check it out.

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.510
Cool. So how do you
show the image preview?

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.530
So once we have the
data uploaded to shiny,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:38.820
how do we show the image?

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:41.570
Well, this is actually just
a Shiny app with one button.

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:43.180
What I do is I have the URL

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:45.210
you go to have the
token, which is

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.600
the ID of the particular
postcard you uploaded,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:50.070
I have that in the URL
and then the Shiny servers

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.440
show--the Shiny app shows a
different postcard depending

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.120
on what your token is.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:57.358
Now, I put a lot of work into
the bootstrap and the CSS

00:06:57.358 --> 00:06:59.400
to make it all look pretty
and to get the margins

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:01.200
on the postcard
right and all that.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.660
I have a whole separate talk
about making your Shiny apps

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:06.450
look pretty that I gave
at Shiny Conf 2022.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:08.850
So if you want like 45
minutes of hearing about how

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:10.350
to make your Shiny
apps look pretty,

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:11.767
I suggest you check
out that talk.

00:07:11.767 --> 00:07:13.500
It was a lot of fun.

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:14.380
So OK, cool.

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:15.700
So we have a Shiny app.

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.742
It shows you the
preview of the thing.

00:07:17.742 --> 00:07:19.200
It got the data
from a POST request

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.312
from R, but how do we then pass
the user from Shiny to Stripe?

00:07:23.312 --> 00:07:25.270
Right, how do I make that
Pay and Submit button

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:26.460
send someone to stripe?

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.070
I thought this would be the
hardest part of the app,

00:07:29.070 --> 00:07:32.700
but it actually was
surprisingly easy to make this.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.400
They're really just two
steps in the payment flow.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:38.725
First is you need Shiny to
send Stripe what is actually

00:07:38.725 --> 00:07:40.350
in the shopping cart
of the user, which

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.650
is called the Stripe session.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:44.350
You do this via a POST
request. By the way,

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:48.210
it turns out ggirl is like
95% just POST requests bouncing

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.780
around and this is another one.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.110
It's not the last.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:55.320
You then also, once you've
POSTed the cart,

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:57.630
you then have to have
the user go to the Shiny

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.200
page or the Stripe page.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.980
So Stripe has a
JavaScript API

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:04.780
to get users to the appropriate
Stripe checkout page.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:06.730
I refuse to learn JavaScript.

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.020
I will never do this.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.750
The good--

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:11.560
Yeah, I'm not alone here.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.730
Great. The good news is, is
that Shiny makes it very easy,

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:19.030
I found out, to use other
people's JavaScript APIs.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.330
So what you do is
you use you add

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:23.430
tags$script to the
top of your Shiny

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:25.560
app to a load in
the JavaScript API

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.840
and then you use
Shiny.addCustomMessageHandler

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.900
so that when the Action button
is pressed for Pay and Submit,

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:33.760
it will call that
special JavaScript.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:35.140
So it's really just two steps.

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:37.223
And again, if you want to
see the particular code,

00:08:37.223 --> 00:08:38.970
you can go to that
ggirl examples repo

00:08:38.970 --> 00:08:41.380
and you can see
an example there.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:44.590
So this gets the user
from Shiny to Stripe

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.870
where they presumably
pay money because they

00:08:46.870 --> 00:08:49.490
are excited about a postcard.

00:08:49.490 --> 00:08:51.700
How do you then
fulfill the order?

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:54.860
So how do you have Shiny then
listen for Stripe striping

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:56.350
like OK, cool, they
paid you money.

00:08:56.350 --> 00:08:58.610
Now we're going to go
ahead and make the order.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:01.340
So we do this with
a POST request!

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:03.590
So we're going to have
Stripe send a POST request

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:05.370
telling Shiny to
order the postcards.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:08.420
So if your server is listening
for someone else's post

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:10.630
request, that is
called a webhook.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:12.862
It's just a different name
for another POST request.

00:09:12.862 --> 00:09:14.820
It's just kind of going
in the other direction.

00:09:14.820 --> 00:09:18.320
So we're going to use a webhook
saying, hey, order xyz,

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:19.290
or fc-6..

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.660
Whatever the order number
is, has been paid for.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.360
And to make sure
that people just

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:25.280
don't know what
that URL is and spam

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:27.050
it to get a million
postcards, we

00:09:27.050 --> 00:09:29.690
are going to add keys to ensure
that the message actually

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.030
came from Stripe itself.

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:35.268
So Stripe will then--so
Shiny will receive the request

00:09:35.268 --> 00:09:37.310
and be like, OK, cool,
the order is placed and then

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.820
Shiny will call the stuff
to send the postcard

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:41.960
and send the email.

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.597
So and this feels like it should
be straightforward, right?

00:09:44.597 --> 00:09:46.430
I've already done two
or three POST requests

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:47.450
as part of this talk.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:49.670
This is just another
POST request.

00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.030
But here's the
issue is that Stripe

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:56.160
needs to know that the message
was received really quickly.

00:09:56.160 --> 00:09:58.040
So if Stripe says the
order was confirmed

00:09:58.040 --> 00:10:00.457
and Stripe doesn't hear right
away that the order actually

00:10:00.457 --> 00:10:02.400
like your app go like,
"Cool, we got the order."

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:04.483
Stripe would be like, "Oh my
god, they didn't hear it.

00:10:04.483 --> 00:10:05.520
I got to send it again."

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:06.978
And here's the
thing you might know

00:10:06.978 --> 00:10:10.020
about Shiny is Shiny takes a
while when it's running stuff.

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:13.230
So if you're not-- ah, you guys
are putting it together.

00:10:13.230 --> 00:10:15.320
Yes, this took me a
while, too long,

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:17.120
to put together that
if you're not careful,

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:19.727
Stripe will ask for the order
to be submitted over and over.

00:10:19.727 --> 00:10:21.560
And Shiny will be like,
"cool, got it. Cool,

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:22.560
got it. Cool, got it."

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:25.070
And the next thing you know,
you have 20 postcards

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:27.280
sent to your house.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:29.200
So the trick to
solve this is you're

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.440
going to use callr package and
callr is a really cool package

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.820
to run R processes
in the background.

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:36.940
So when you get the
order from Stripe,

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.670
you're going to use callr
to actually send

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:40.925
the postcard, send the email.

00:10:40.925 --> 00:10:42.800
That's all going to
happen in the background.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.440
So that Shiny can
immediately respond

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.273
to Stripe being like,
"Yeah, cool, we got it."

00:10:46.273 --> 00:10:47.710
And not be bogged
down by the time

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.680
it takes to send the
postcard and to submit

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:52.780
the postcard order
and send the email.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.870
I love using callr so much from
learning this that I actually

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.083
made a whole talk about
using R in parallel,

00:10:58.083 --> 00:11:00.500
which is a separate thing if
you want to learn about that.

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:02.460
I have a separate talk for this.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:04.960
And if you actually want to
just see the code that does this,

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.270
again, ggirl-examples has that.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:10.420
So to actually fulfill the
order I needed to use callr

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:13.200
to run R in the background.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:14.800
And then to order the postcards.

00:11:14.800 --> 00:11:16.950
It turns out there are
tons of companies out there

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:20.190
that will make APIs that if
you hit that API with a POST

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.740
request, with a message,
the address and the front

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:25.157
of the postcard, they will
ship it to that location.

00:11:25.157 --> 00:11:27.240
This makes sense because
you get lots of postcards

00:11:27.240 --> 00:11:29.430
in the mail for advertisements,
so lots of companies

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.050
use these services.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:32.250
Two problems here.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:34.440
One, it takes a while to
ship postcards, right?

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:36.190
It takes like two weeks
to ship postcards.

00:11:36.190 --> 00:11:39.050
So it takes a while before you
actually get the postcard back.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:41.790
And two, companies that are
sending millions of postcards

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:44.092
to every person in the
country don't care really

00:11:44.092 --> 00:11:46.300
how good the postcards look
as long as they're cheap.

00:11:46.300 --> 00:11:48.570
So it took forever for
me to find a service that

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:49.412
was actually good.

00:11:49.412 --> 00:11:51.870
And so it took two weeks and
I didn't like it and two weeks

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:52.745
and I didn't like it.

00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:54.300
And in fact, if
you see over there,

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:56.610
if you see in this image
on the presentation,

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:58.150
it looks kind of blurry.

00:11:58.150 --> 00:11:59.873
It looks kind of
like JPEG artifacts.

00:11:59.873 --> 00:12:01.290
Like this was a
Facebook meme that

00:12:01.290 --> 00:12:02.710
got sent to you too many times.

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.810
That was not this
picture I uploaded.

00:12:04.810 --> 00:12:07.060
That's what it actually
looked like on the postcard.

00:12:07.060 --> 00:12:11.760
And that is beneath the
dignity of ggplot2 plots.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:14.300
So I had to spend a while,
by far this the longest

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:16.800
part, because I had to spend a
while until I find a printing

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:19.090
service that was actually good.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.442
To send the confirmation emails.

00:12:21.442 --> 00:12:22.650
This is actually pretty easy.

00:12:22.650 --> 00:12:25.450
There's a package blastula
which can send those emails.

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:27.700
So all I have to do to put
the preview in the email is

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:30.460
I wanted to resize it because
those images had to be very

00:12:30.460 --> 00:12:32.110
large for the
printer and I wanted

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.790
to resize them so they weren't
like five megs in your inbox.

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:37.570
So I use the package image
magic to resize them and get

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:39.160
the right margins
and everything,

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.410
but sometimes there would be
an error in the fulfillment.

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:45.010
So not only did I have to
send an email to the users,

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:46.650
but if anything
ever goes wrong, I

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:47.900
want to make sure I caught it.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:49.870
So I'd have to send an
email to me and once

00:12:49.870 --> 00:12:51.250
in a while the
email system would

00:12:51.250 --> 00:12:53.780
have an error in emailing, so
I had to email about that too.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:56.680
It was a real web of
emails being sent.

00:12:56.680 --> 00:12:59.740
So lastly, how do you
actually deploy it?

00:12:59.740 --> 00:13:04.100
So I needed the server to
actually be--that ggirl-server,

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.950
the Shiny app had to be
continuously living somewhere.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.947
So the place that it is currently
running is on Saturn Cloud.

00:13:09.947 --> 00:13:12.530
So Saturn Cloud, I'm the chief
product officer of the company.

00:13:12.530 --> 00:13:13.905
And so it's really
nice for this.

00:13:13.905 --> 00:13:16.060
I know it's a--

00:13:16.060 --> 00:13:18.077
I'm an advertiser.

00:13:18.077 --> 00:13:19.660
So it was actually
very nice because I

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:23.210
have two RStudio servers running
in Saturn Cloud in the cloud.

00:13:23.210 --> 00:13:24.020
So one was the one

00:13:24.020 --> 00:13:25.150
I actually was
showing you, which

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.250
is how I did the development
of the Shiny package

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:28.670
in RStudio in a browser.

00:13:28.670 --> 00:13:32.740
But I also had a RStudio running
for developing the Shiny back

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:35.620
end, and then I actually
have two Shiny servers

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:38.710
running on Saturn Cloud. One,
the actual server, the Shiny

00:13:38.710 --> 00:13:40.990
server, you saw the
production one, but as well as

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:42.460
the development
one that I could

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:44.470
test with and use
like fake credit cards

00:13:44.470 --> 00:13:46.090
and it would fake send emails.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:47.710
So I ended up having
four systems all

00:13:47.710 --> 00:13:49.627
running on Saturn Cloud,
which is pretty nice.

00:13:49.627 --> 00:13:50.300
And easy.

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:52.450
And most importantly,
I beta tested this

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:54.580
with a bunch of people to
make sure it worked first

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:57.070
and that took a while and
helped me really iron out

00:13:57.070 --> 00:14:00.670
some usability issues and
that was really nice as well.

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:04.300
So wrapping it all up,
ggirl is a cool package

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:06.563
for ordering cool stuff.

00:14:06.563 --> 00:14:08.230
This required me to
do a bunch of things

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.055
that I assumed might
be true in Shiny,

00:14:10.055 --> 00:14:11.180
but I didn't actually know.

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:12.138
And it turns out it is.

00:14:12.138 --> 00:14:14.410
Which includes receiving
data from an R package,

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:17.560
listening for POST requests,
sending and receiving

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:21.070
Stripe API calls and spawning
background processes that

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.880
didn't slow down the
Shiny responsiveness.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:25.110
I forgot to mention.

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:26.760
So thank you.

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:28.260
I forgot to mention
at the beginning

00:14:28.260 --> 00:14:29.850
that not only can
you send ggpostcards,

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.040
but you can also order R prints
for your walls, which people

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:33.750
have used for like graduation
presents and stuff, which

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:34.390
is really cool.

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:34.970
And people use.

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:36.900
Oh, by the way, people use like
ggpostcards for like baby

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:37.970
announcements and stuff.

00:14:37.970 --> 00:14:39.670
People get really
clever uses for these.

00:14:39.670 --> 00:14:40.605
So it's a lot of fun.

00:14:40.605 --> 00:14:43.230
And there's ggwatercolor(), which is
just I hand-paint a watercolor

00:14:43.230 --> 00:14:44.700
for you if you want it.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:48.290
So you can install the
package with the ggnolis--

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:49.930
that's not me.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:52.355
jnolis/ggirl, you can
actually, if you

00:14:52.355 --> 00:14:54.480
want to make one of your
own souvenir commemorative

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:56.827
postcards, the code
is linked above.

00:14:56.827 --> 00:14:59.160
The examples of the code in
practice from the production

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:00.585
server are at the ggirl-examples.

00:15:00.585 --> 00:15:02.710
And if you want to see the
slides, that's the link.

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:05.610
But lastly, I have a whole
bunch of coupons for 80% off,

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:08.058
which means you can get a
postcard sent for $0.50,

00:15:08.058 --> 00:15:10.350
which is the cheapest Stripe
will allow me to do before

00:15:10.350 --> 00:15:11.955
saying I can't do that.

00:15:11.955 --> 00:15:14.580
So if you come talk to me after
I have so many of these coupons

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:17.080
to give out because I'm really
excited for people to use us.

00:15:17.080 --> 00:15:19.190
So OK. Thank you.

